it's all about the benjamins baby  these benjamins of course 100 bills  adorned with a portrait of benjamin  franklin but have you ever wondered why  it's  his face on the bill or why we put faces  of presidents  and some non-presidents on american  money well dear viewers  i will be answering that very question  as i explain the history of the people  on the money of these united states  money has been around for about as long  as the civilization has existed  before middle coins many different  things were used as money  like clay tablets beads and shells  [Music]  the first metal coins were from lydia an  ancient civilization in modern day  turkey  the usefulness durability and popularity  of these coins  caused other neighboring civilizations  like the greeks  to adopt the practice it wasn't long  before the faces of powerful people  like kings and emperors adorned these  coins  after america's founding the opinion was  that  presidents or other founding fathers  shouldn't be put on the money  we had just broken from a monarchy and  didn't want to adopt  their tradition of putting leaders on  the coins  so for the first almost 150 years  of the republic no presidents were  featured on our coinage  instead lady liberty a symbol of freedom  and democracy  was on the front of coins while the  american eagle  was on the back until finally in the  year 1909 the 100th  anniversary of abraham lincoln's birth  president teddy roosevelt  championed an initiative to put lincoln  on the penny  the design was based on a sculpture by  victor  david brenner which is why the bust of  lincoln is facing the opposite direction  from everyone else it was originally  going to be  commemorative and only last one year  but the lincoln penny proved so popular  with the public that the u.s  mint decided to keep it ever since  27 years later in 1932 the bicentennial  celebration of washington's birth  george washington got his turn to be on  a coin  this time the quarter again it was  designed to be only for a year  but was extended to this day due to its  popularity  with these two examples the floodgates  were opened in 1938  thomas jefferson the third president and  writer of the declaration of  independence was put on the nickel in  1946  after the second world war and president  frederick delano roosevelt's death  there was a grassroots campaign to get  him on a coin and the us mint was more  than happy to put him  on the dime after the assassination of  president john f kennedy in  1963 the mint wasted no time  in putting his portrait on the half  dollar which is a coin we don't see too  often  speaking of dollar coins have not been  particularly popular in the united  states  but they are made and are still in  circulation  in 1971 dwight d eisenhower was put on a  dollar coin  but it only ran until 1978. susan b  anthony was also put on a dollar coin  but the coins were somewhat unpopular  because of their confusion with quarters  so it was replaced by the current dollar  coin  which features a portrait of saku joaya  the native american woman who guided  lewis and clark on their famous  exploration  moving on to notes the first paper money  was created in the tang dynasty in china  during the 7th  century so by the time the united states  was founded  paper money had been around for  centuries but surprisingly the federal  government of the us  didn't start printing bank notes until  1861  almost 100 years after the country's  founding before that point  private banks were permitted to simply  print their own notes  so there were dozens if not hundreds of  different paper currencies in the us at  the time  as i said the us government started  printing greenbacks  named so because of the green ink used  in  1861 as a way of paying for expenses  related to the civil war at this point  though it was before any president ended  up on a coin the american people seemed  fine  with the idea of putting a president or  other political leaders on the bills  the first u.s bank notes had some of the  classics  like george washington and alexander  hamilton  but it also had some surprises like  abraham lincoln who of course we're used  to seeing  now but keep in mind he was still the  president at the time  and of course you all know salmon  portland chase  no well he was the treasury secretary  under lincoln so i guess that makes  sense  as time went on there were a great  number of people who showed up on the  bills  many presidents senators cabinet members  and so on  two noteworthy people i want to mention  were martha washington  george washington's wife and first first  lady of the united states  she to this day is the only woman to  show up on a u.s banknote  and running antelope a sioux chief of  the  hong papa indians he is the only native  american  and person of color to show up on  american paper money  man that is a bit embarrassing in 1928  the current slate of portraits on  american paper money was solidified  washington on the one jefferson on the  two  lincoln on the five hamilton on the 10  jackson on the 20 grant on the 50  and franklin on the 100. it was  confirmed in 2016 that harriet tubman  the great abolitionist and political  activist would be replacing andrew  jackson on the  20 bill becoming the first  african-american woman  and first black american period to be  featured on american paper money  the change was set to happen in 2020 but  unfortunately  it has been postponed until 2028.  at that time the 10 and 50 notes will  also have redesigns  so we will all have to wait and see  hello thanks for watching my video about  american money  please like the video if you liked it  please subscribe if you want to be  updated every time  i upload a new video and consider  sharing the video with your friends  and check out some of my other videos if  you want to learn more  bye
 

